==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 15 NOTES — Project 2: Capture Your Lab Traffic
Date: 14-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- A capture filter (tcpdump 'tcp port 8080') keeps unrelated traffic out of the file; a display filter only hides it
- tcpdump -w saves packets, tcpdump -r reads them back, and the same file opens in Wireshark and tshark
- tshark -T fields -e <field> prints just the fields I ask for, which turns a capture into a fact sheet
- A report is only worth something if every finding points to a command or screenshot
- A good report also says what it does NOT show

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] tcpdump -i lo -n 'tcp port 8080' -w web.pcap
[ ] tcpdump -i eth0 -n 'icmp or port 53' -w net.pcap
[ ] tcpdump -r web.pcap with a SYN filter
[ ] capinfos / tshark -z conv,tcp / tshark -T fields
[ ] filled in REPORT_TEMPLATE.md

